  "versions": [
    {
      "meaning": "In biblical tradition, the number 20 symbolizes a period of waiting, testing, and maturity.",
      "description": "Jacob waited 20 years while serving Laban before being released, and the Israelites were oppressed for 20 years under Jabin, king of Canaan, before Deborah and Barak delivered them (Judges 4:3). In Jewish tradition, 20 years was considered the age of maturity and responsibility, as men from 20 years and older were mustered for military service (Numbers 1:3). The number is thus linked to a transition from imperfection to full responsibility and deliverance after a period of testing.",
      "reason": "[minor] The description is generally good but lacks an explicit reference to Jacob's 20 years with Laban (Genesis 31:38-41). A Bible reference here would strengthen the text.; [minor] There are several interesting details about the number 20 that could enrich the text with footnotes, for example, Solomon's temple took 20 years to build (1 Kings 9:10), and Samson judged Israel for 20 years (Judges 15:20). Rabbinic tradition regarding 20 as the age of majority for heavenly judgment could also be added as a footnote.; [minor] The meaning field is comprehensive but could include the aspect of military age of majority more explicitly, since this is a central aspect of the number.",
      "score": 7,
      "date": "2026-04-04"
    }
  ],
  "footnotes": [
    {
      "text": "In rabbinic tradition (Talmud, Shabbat 89b), 20 years is regarded as the age when a person becomes accountable before the heavenly court. While earthly accountability (bar mitzvah) begins at 13 years, full heavenly accountability is considered to start only from 20 years.",
      "source": "rabbinsk"
    },
    {
      "text": "All Israelites under 20 years of age at the Exodus from Egypt were allowed to enter the Promised Land, while those over 20 who had participated in the rebellion upon the spies' return died in the wilderness (Numbers 14:29). The number 20 thus served as a legal boundary for divine judgment.",
      "source": "teologisk"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Hebrew word for 20, עֶשְׂרִים (esrim), is the plural form of עֶשֶׂר (eser, ten). The number 20 can therefore be understood as a doubling of 10, which itself symbolizes completeness and divine order.",
      "source": "lingvistisk"
    }
  ]
}
